{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481943,"nonce":"kB4y30HjTp/+oa7IGmBTmdyNWIc=","title":"**What is wrong.** The notification view has two ways to learn who caused a fact and what they said, and **neither is ever written in production**. `ViewRequest.Actor` (`internal/notification/service/view.go:73`) and `ViewRequest.Comment` (`:75`) are read by `project` at `:133` and `:107`; both production callers of `Build` construct a `ViewRequest` with only `Notification` set. The fallback at `:135` reads `domain.Snapshot.Actor` (`internal/notification/domain/snapshot.go:285`), which the snapshot repository never scans into, and `Snapshot.Comment` (`:286`) is in the same state. So `NotificationView.Actor` is always `nil` and `NotificationView.Comment` is always `\"\"`, in every deployment, on every card.","message":"**The demonstration.** Four links, each verifiable on its own line.\n\n- `internal/notification/service/dispatch.go:558` — `view, err := s.views.Build(ctx, scope, ViewRequest{Notification: n})`. No `Actor`, no `Comment`. `internal/notification/api/preview.go:185` is the second caller and sets neither.\n- `internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go` fills `Snapshot` field by field and never touches `Actor` or `Comment`. `readOrg` at `:274` scans `\u0026snap.Org.ID, \u0026snap.Org.Slug, \u0026snap.Org.Name`; the trail scan at `:254` reads `\u0026f.Type, \u0026f.At, \u0026f.ActorLabel` — the per-transition label on a `TransitionFact`, not the notification's actor.\n- The one place an actor enters the module dies one hop in. `internal/notification/worker/worker.go:116` sets `Actor: args.Actor` on the `Intent`; `Intent.Actor` is declared at `internal/notification/service/notify.go:38` and read by nothing. `Evaluate` neither forwards nor persists it.\n- `actorLabel` therefore returns `\"\"` on its nil check at `internal/channels/render/slack/renderer.go:249-251`, and every consumer degrades. In `replyBody` (`reply.go:84`), `who := actorLabel(v)` at `:86` feeds `by(who)` at `:91`, `:98` and `:153` — so an acknowledgement thread reply is permanently `\":eyes: *Acknowledged*\"` with no name, an un-acknowledgement the same, a silence the same. On the root card, `statusValue` at `root.go:593` and `:597` guards on `who != \"\"` and so never appends `\" by …\"` to `Acknowledged` or `Suppressed`. Worst is `reply.go:175`: `body = \":speech_balloon: \" + commentPrefix(who) + escape(oneLine(v.Comment))`, and with `who == \"\"` `commentPrefix` returns `\"\"` (`reply.go:312-317`) — a `comment` notification renders as a speech-balloon emoji and nothing else.\n\nOne consumer does survive, which is why this is easy to miss by eye: `acknowledgedValue` at `root.go:181-186` falls back to `v.Occurrence.AckedByLabel`, which *is* populated (`snapshot.go:386` → `view.go:335`). So the root card's Acknowledged **field** names the acker while the same card's status line and the matching thread reply do not. The inconsistency is the tell.\n\nlintreach found all of it and recorded it as **seven unrelated debts**: `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt:269-271` (`ActorFacts.ID`/`.Kind`/`.Label`), `:274-275` (`Snapshot.Actor`, `Snapshot.Comment`), `:285-286` (`ViewRequest.Actor`, `ViewRequest.Comment`) and `:156` (`Intent.Actor` — *\"field is never read in production — the value goes nowhere\"*). Seven lines, one defect.\n\n**Why it matters.** The actor field is the mechanism CONTEXT.md §1b makes the *only* sanctioned way a person may appear on a signal: *\"Human actions are IN **only** as timestamped annotations on a signal's timeline, carrying an actor as *metadata*. In one line: **ACTOR, NEVER SUBJECT.**\"* The channel exists, is documented, is rendered — and carries nothing.\n\nThe comment case is worse than cosmetic. CONTEXT.md §6 records that retention *\"deletes the **narrative**, never the **record**\"* and that *\"What dies at 13 months is the timeline, including human comments, which live nowhere else.\"* A comment whose text never reaches the card is not a display bug: it is a human's words going into a system that then shows an empty balloon to the channel they typed into.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/service/view.go:73`, `:75`, `:107`, `:133-139`; `internal/notification/service/dispatch.go:558`; `internal/notification/api/preview.go:185`; `internal/notification/service/notify.go:38`; `internal/notification/worker/worker.go:116`; `internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go:254`, `:274`; `internal/notification/domain/snapshot.go:285-286`; `internal/channels/render/slack/renderer.go:248-262`; `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go:86`, `:91`, `:98`, `:153`, `:175`, `:312-317`; `internal/channels/render/slack/root.go:593`, `:597`; `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt:156`, `:269-271`, `:274-275`, `:285-286`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `Evaluate` persists the intent's actor and the comment text (or the snapshot query reads both back from `alert_events`), both `Build` call sites pass them through, a Slack golden shows a named actor on the ack reply *and* in the root card's status line, another shows a non-empty body on a `comment` reply, and the seven baseline lines above fail as stale.","files":null}]}